		{
			"id": "112158105936232020",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://front-end.social/@a11yMel/112158105908994153",
			"author": {
				"name": "Melanie",
				"url": "https://front-end.social/@a11yMel",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/349/997/004/476/619/original/0a852b5a247738c3.jpeg"
			},
			"date": "2024-03-25T20:01:19.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> surely it depends. An app used internally at a large corporation should not have to worry about worse-case fast. Heavy duty apps can do heavy duty lifting on networks created for that purpose. </p><p>Any public website absolutely should be worst-case fast though. </p><p>I don’t know where webdevs work or why we seem so incapable of this nuance. It can be both. It depends.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
