			"date": "2024-03-25T20:21:00.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://front-end.social/@a11yMel\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>a11yMel</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Perhaps that statement about internal corporate apps is true if there is no remote work, no satellite offices in areas with less-good Internet, and corporate users are always on recently upgraded machines which prioritize speed over battery life or portability. But none of these assumptions is especially true of most corporations, especially these days.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
