		{
			"id": "112158320073047350",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://front-end.social/@benschwarz/112158320044538593",
			"author": {
				"name": "benschwarz",
				"url": "https://front-end.social/@benschwarz",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/295/713/321/949/633/original/45e52f3c0901216e.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-03-25T20:55:47.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> testing the full spectrum of slow device / slow network to ‘fastest possible’ has been a mainstay in messaging from me <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://webperf.social/@calibre\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>calibre</span></a></span></p><p>However, there are still a lot of teams out there who intentionally hide worst case scenarios because it’s so bad and represents too much work to improve. </p><p>They’re so busy with the best case, that poor experiences don’t get a look  in, which is totally counterintuitive because if you make a page fast for a slow phone, it’ll FLY everywhere else.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
